[Music]  [Music]  despite the countless dragon ball games  that have appeared since the manga  debuted in the mid 80s  the series has never needed them to  sustain its popularity most are  forgettable some are good but even fewer  are truly great thanks to developer Arc  System Works particular talents  Dragonball Fighters is one of the great  ones if not the best yet whether still  or in motion fighters art looks like  Dragonball at it's very best  adhering closely to the standards set by  series creator Akira Toriyama and no  matter how you may have watched the show  the option to choose between Japanese  and English voice acting makes it easy  to feel connected to the events on  screen you can't let your guard down  even if you've already won but then the  convincing Dragon Ball shell is a fast  paced 3v3 tag-team fighting game that  will feel familiar to Marvel vs. Capcom  3 veterans but despite a few familiar  parallels fighters is distinctly  Dragonball every character can jet  through the air in a flash at any time  toss energy blasts like it's nothing and  unleash a flurry of small punches and  kicks to stagger a hesitant opponent  these super beings are incredibly  powerful and fighters translates that  energy to the screen perfectly  it also makes it easy for anyone to tap  into that power with relatively short  special attack lists and one button or  two button activations for Universal  mechanics like any great fighting game  fighters doesn't lose depth just because  it's accessible super attacks and  teleports are easy to pull off but they  come with timing and combo conditions  that allow for expert level analysis and  strategic play it's also important to  properly manage the lone meter that  fuels most of your special abilities a  setup that makes fighters next move more  unpredictable than usual compared to  some games with multiple of building  specific meters  [Music]  of course you also don't have to be an  aspiring online competitor to enjoy  fighters as it includes a significant  story mode that can last a dozen hours  or more if you seek out every possible  cutscene while a [ __ ] run out in places  and relatively easy until the conclusion  it's still a treat for Dragon Ball fans  with plenty of new vignettes starring  classic characters Dragon Ball super  characters on the roster notwithstanding  but the plot is split into three arcs  you are technically seeing one arc from  different perspectives with a few  alternate events to keep things  interesting because there's practically  zero time spent introducing you to the  characters or their world it's difficult  to imagine how a newcomer to Dragon Ball  would understand things like the gimme  forces proclivity to pose dramatically  or the reason why Crillon doesn't have a  nose let alone the broad concepts of  Super Saiyans and Dragon Balls then  again  the mix of oddball antics and hyper  serious face offs is inherently  appealing for the confident cartoon  expression on display which requires no  additional context to enjoy as in combat  arcs capable design skills make the 3d  models and environments and cutscenes  looks stunningly close to actual 2d  animation there are moments when you  feel like you're watching a new episode  of Dragon Ball Z but there is a catch  you're forced to press a button to  advance dialogue rather than allowed to  kick back and watch the show when  fighters gets a colon close to  recreating the look of the anime the  forced interaction feels like a step in  the wrong direction albeit a minor one  in the grand scheme of things story  modes real downfalls how repetitive it  becomes you fight clones of only a  portion of the games overall roster ad  nauseam each chapter is presented like a  map locations connected by a branching  path in order to get to the chapter boss  you have to navigate the board and pick  and choose your fights along the way  given that there are optional pathways  in each chapter and that you can concoct  your own team it's not surprising to  learn that there are optional cutscenes  to unlock depending on these conditions  despite the rewards being largely  enjoyable after a handful of hours  fighting lackluster opponents the idea  of replaying story chapters to see a  quirky character interaction is  unfortunately one that's  to sideline similarly the game's basic  small over world feels unnecessary even  though it attempts to add value modes  are divided among spokes around a  circular hub and you can run around as  small versions of the games characters  sometimes an alternate outfits the game  tries to earn your interest throughout  lockable avatars but even if this sounds  good you can only earn them through  randomized loot boxes you earn money as  you fight in complete story mode  milestones and these can be cashed in  for a capsule which turns into a random  cosmetic item there unfortunately no  alternate costumes available for combat  rather than harm the game this system  just feels unnecessary as none of the  rewards are critical to enjoying what  matters most participating in explosive  battles and enjoying interactions  between dragon balls lovably bizarre  characters though merely a small piece  of the overall puzzle the rare dramatic  finishes are perhaps the most  respectable and impressive nod to fans  in fighters anyone who spent years  watching Dragon Ball Z unfold over  nearly 300 episodes will gasp the first  time they trigger one which will only  happen with certain matchups on her  particular conditions they have nothing  to do with fighters story but they have  everything to do with the revered  history of the series at large  [Music]  surely after release been an AAMCO  servers were inundated with eager  players to the point that it was often  difficult to get into a lobby at all  this no longer seems to be an issue  thankfully though even when servers  behave as they should the hub world at  the center of it all proves to make  matchmaking with friends an even more  complicated process than it ought to be  rather than simply inviting a friend  into a match you have to coordinate to  make sure you're both in the same server  and the same Lobby before finding each  other's avatars and creating a private  match lock of the password it doesn't  take long to get used to but it's also  another sign that perhaps the hub world  is an unnecessary complication online  quirks aside fighters is built to  satisfy demanding fighting game  competitors but there's no question that  it's been designed to tap into the  hearts of Dragon Balls most dedicated  fans and no doubt that those same  qualities will win people over who've  never given the series a chance where  past games attempted to get there  through huge rosters and deliberately  predictable trips down memory lane  fighters has bottled the essence of what  makes the series characters animation  and sense of humor so beloved and a  reconfigured it into something new a  Dragon Ball fighting game that can go  toe-to-toe with the best of the job  [Music]

